helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3154524				0	gl	GCD(Integer1; Integer2; ...; Integer30)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3149340				0	gl	\<emph\>Integer1 To 30\</emph\> are up to 30 integers whose greatest common divisor is to be calculated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147317				0	gl	Exemplo				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3151285				0	gl	\<item type=\"input\"\>=GCD(16;32;24) \</item\>gives the result 8, because 8 is the largest number that can divide 16, 24 and 32 without a remainder.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id1604663				0	gl	\<item type=\"input\"\>=GCD(B1:B3)\</item\> where cells B1, B2, B3 contain \<item type=\"input\"\>9\</item\>, \<item type=\"input\"\>12\</item\>, \<item type=\"input\"\>9\</item\> gives 3.				20200411 15:09:53
